content:
Dana 12. maja 2026. godine, u JU Srednja elektro-ekonomska škola u Bijelom Polju realizovana je šesta edukativna radionica u okviru projekta „Glas mladih za dostojanstven rad – od znanja do promjene“, usmjerena na osnaživanje mladih kroz unapređenje znanja o radnim pravima, dostojanstvenom radu, zaštiti i zdravlju na radu, kao i pripremu učenika za bezbjedniji i odgovorniji ulazak na tržište rada.
Radionici je prisustvovalo 79 učenika, što ovu aktivnost čini jednom od najposjećenijih edukativnih radionica realizovanih u okviru projekta. Učešće učenika elektro smjera i smjera ekonomski tehničar omogućilo je realizaciju edukacije prilagođene specifičnostima dva potpuno različita profesionalna okruženja, čime je radionica dobila dodatnu praktičnu vrijednost i relevantnost za same učesnike.
Edukaciju su realizovali Đina Janković i Branislav Šebek iz Udruženja zaštite na radu Crne Gore, kroz interaktivan pristup koji je kombinovao stručnu edukaciju, praktične primjere, diskusiju i aktivno uključivanje učenika, sa ciljem da se teorijska znanja povežu sa konkretnim izazovima sa kojima će se mladi suočavati u svojim budućim profesijama.
Značaj edukacije u kontekstu različitih profesionalnih profila
Specifičnost ove radionice ogleda se u činjenici da su učesnici dolazili iz obrazovnih profila koji vode ka značajno različitim profesionalnim sektorima, sa različitim uslovima rada i vrstama profesionalnih rizika.
Upravo iz tog razloga, edukacija je koncipirana tako da, pored opštih tema iz oblasti radnih prava i zaštite i zdravlja na radu, poseban fokus stavi na specifične rizike karakteristične za profesije za koje se učenici obrazuju.
Za učenike smjera ekonomski tehničar, akcenat je stavljen na rizike kancelarijskog rada, ergonomiju i značaj pravilne organizacije radnog mjesta, dok je za učenike elektro smjera poseban fokus bio na profesionalnim rizicima karakterističnim za sektor energetike i tehničkih zanimanja.
Ovakav pristup omogućio je učesnicima da edukaciju dožive kao direktno relevantnu za sopstvenu profesionalnu budućnost, a ne kao apstraktnu ili opštu temu.
Dostojanstven rad i radna prava kao osnova sigurnog zaposlenja
U uvodnom dijelu radionice predstavljeni su ciljevi projekta „Glas mladih za dostojanstven rad – od znanja do promjene“, uz poseban osvrt na položaj mladih na tržištu rada i izazove sa kojima se suočavaju pri prvom zaposlenju.
Naglašeno je da mladi često na tržište rada ulaze bez dovoljno informacija o svojim pravima, obavezama i mehanizmima zaštite, što ih čini posebno ranjivima na:
• rad bez ugovora;
• nesigurne oblike angažovanja;
• neplaćeni ili neformalni rad;
• neprijavljivanje zaposlenja;
• izloženost nesigurnim i neadekvatnim uslovima rada.
• Kroz edukativni segment obrađene su teme:
• koncept dostojanstvenog rada;
• osnovna prava i obaveze iz radnog odnosa;
• značaj formalnog zaposlenja;
• pravo na bezbjedne uslove rada;
• odgovornost poslodavca;
• značaj informisanosti i preventivne kulture.
Posebno je naglašeno da dostojanstven rad ne znači samo mogućnost zaposlenja, već i pravo na rad u uslovima koji štite zdravlje, bezbjednost i ljudsko dostojanstvo zaposlenih.
Specifični fokus – kancelarijski rad i ergonomija
Za učenike koji se obrazuju za buduća zanimanja u administrativnom i ekonomskom sektoru, poseban fokus bio je na izazovima savremenog kancelarijskog rada, koji se često pogrešno doživljava kao profesionalno „bezbjedan“ sektor.
Kroz edukaciju je ukazano na činjenicu da kancelarijski poslovi nose niz profesionalnih rizika koji mogu imati ozbiljne dugoročne posljedice po zdravlje, uključujući:
• dugotrajno sjedenje i statičko opterećenje mišićno-koštanog sistema;
• nepravilno ergonomski podešeno radno mjesto;
• zamor vida usljed kontinuiranog rada za ekranom;
• bolove u vratu, ramenima i leđima;
• psihičko opterećenje i stres;
• neadekvatne mikroklimatske uslove rada.
Posebna pažnja posvećena je značaju ergonomije, pravilnog položaja tijela tokom rada, adekvatnog rasporeda opreme i preventivnih mjera koje doprinose očuvanju zdravlja i radne sposobnosti.
Specifični fokus – profesionalni rizici u sektoru energetike
Za učenike elektro smjera, edukacija je bila usmjerena na prepoznavanje ozbiljnih profesionalnih rizika karakterističnih za tehničke i energetske sektore, koji spadaju među profesionalno zahtjevnija i rizičnija radna okruženja.
Obrađene su teme koje se odnose na:
⚡ električni udar i kontakt sa instalacijama pod naponom;
🔥 opasnosti od požara i eksplozija;
🏗️ rad na visini;
🛠️ povrede pri radu sa alatima i opremom;
⚙️ mehaničke povrede;
👂 izloženost buci i vibracijama;
🧪 potencijalna izloženost štetnim materijama;
🦺 značaj lične zaštitne opreme;
📋 procjenu rizika i bezbjedne procedure rada.
Naglašeno je da u tehničkim zanimanjima jedan trenutak nepažnje ili nepoštovanja procedura može imati ozbiljne posljedice, zbog čega kultura prevencije i odgovornosti mora biti sastavni dio profesionalnog identiteta budućih radnika u ovom sektoru.
Aktivno učešće i interesovanje učenika
Jedan od posebno značajnih aspekata ove radionice bio je visok nivo zainteresovanosti učesnika i aktivno uključivanje tokom edukacije.
Učenici su pokazali veliko interesovanje za praktične teme koje su direktno povezane sa njihovim budućim zanimanjima, postavljali konkretna pitanja i aktivno učestvovali u diskusiji.
Posebno je bilo evidentno da učenici prepoznaju značaj tema koje se odnose na bezbjednost na radu kada se one predstave kroz kontekst njihove konkretne profesije i realnih situacija iz prakse.
Mehanizmi podrške mladima
U okviru radionice predstavljen je i Help-line servis / online Pravni kutak za mlade, kao mehanizam podrške putem kojeg mladi mogu dobiti informacije, savjete i stručnu podršku u vezi sa radnim pravima i pitanjima zaštite i zdravlja na radu.
Naglašeno je da je cilj ovog servisa da mladima pruži siguran i dostupan kanal podrške, posebno u situacijama kada se suoče sa nejasnoćama, nepravilnostima ili potencijalnim povredama svojih prava.
Radionica realizovana u JU Srednja elektro-ekonomska škola u Bijelom Polju predstavljala je posebno vrijednu aktivnost u okviru projekta, upravo zbog uspješnog povezivanja opštih tema radnih prava i dostojanstvenog rada sa specifičnim profesionalnim rizicima relevantnim za različite obrazovne profile učesnika.
Veliki broj učesnika, kvalitet interakcije i izraženo interesovanje učenika potvrđuju da postoji stvarna potreba za ovakvim edukativnim sadržajima u srednjim stručnim školama, kako bi mladi stekli znanja koja će im omogućiti sigurniji, informisaniji i odgovorniji ulazak na tržište rada.
Ova aktivnost još jednom potvrđuje da ulaganje u znanje mladih predstavlja jednu od najvažnijih preventivnih mjera u izgradnji sigurnijeg, zdravijeg i dostojanstvenijeg radnog okruženja za buduće generacije.
Projekat finansira Evropska unija, a kofinansira Ministarstvo regionalno-investicionog razvoja i saradnje sa nevladinim organizacijama, u okviru mreže YHWBT.

content_en:
On 12 May 2026, at the Secondary School of Electrical Engineering and Economics in Bijelo Polje, the sixth educational workshop under the project “Youth voices for decent work – from knowledge to change” was held, aimed at empowering young people by improving their knowledge of labour rights, decent work and occupational safety and health, and at preparing pupils for a safer and more responsible entry into the labour market.
The workshop was attended by 79 pupils, making it one of the best-attended educational workshops held under the project. The participation of pupils from both the electrical engineering and the economics technician streams made it possible to tailor the training to two entirely different professional environments, which gave the workshop added practical value and relevance for the participants themselves.
The training was delivered by Đina Janković and Branislav Šebek of the Occupational Safety Association of Montenegro, through an interactive approach combining expert instruction, practical examples, discussion and the active involvement of the pupils, with the aim of connecting theoretical knowledge to the concrete challenges young people will face in their future occupations.
The importance of education in the context of different professional profiles
What made this workshop distinctive is that the participants came from educational profiles leading to significantly different professional sectors, with different working conditions and different kinds of occupational risk.
For that reason the training was designed so that, alongside general topics in labour rights and occupational safety and health, particular focus was placed on the specific risks characteristic of the occupations the pupils are training for.
For pupils on the economics technician stream, the emphasis was on the risks of office work, on ergonomics and on the importance of organising a workstation properly, while for pupils on the electrical engineering stream the particular focus was on the occupational risks characteristic of the energy sector and technical occupations.
This approach allowed participants to experience the training as directly relevant to their own professional future, rather than as an abstract or general topic.
Decent work and labour rights as the basis of secure employment
The opening part of the workshop presented the aims of the project “Youth voices for decent work – from knowledge to change”, with particular reference to the position of young people on the labour market and the challenges they face in their first job.
It was emphasised that young people often enter the labour market without enough information about their rights, their obligations and the mechanisms available to protect them, which leaves them particularly vulnerable to:
• work without a contract;
• insecure forms of engagement;
• unpaid or informal work;
• employment that is not registered;
• exposure to unsafe and inadequate working conditions.
• The educational segment covered:
• the concept of decent work;
• basic rights and obligations arising from an employment relationship;
• the importance of formal employment;
• the right to safe working conditions;
• the employer's responsibility;
• the importance of being informed and of a preventive culture.
It was particularly emphasised that decent work means not only the opportunity to be employed, but also the right to work in conditions that protect employees' health, safety and human dignity.
Specific focus – office work and ergonomics
For pupils training for future occupations in the administrative and economic sector, particular focus was placed on the challenges of modern office work, which is often wrongly seen as a professionally “safe” sector.
The training pointed out that office work carries a range of occupational risks that can have serious long-term consequences for health, including:
• prolonged sitting and static load on the musculoskeletal system;
• a workstation that is not ergonomically adjusted;
• eye strain from continuous work at a screen;
• pain in the neck, shoulders and back;
• mental strain and stress;
• inadequate indoor climate conditions.
Particular attention was paid to the importance of ergonomics, correct body posture during work, the appropriate arrangement of equipment and the preventive measures that help preserve health and the capacity to work.
Specific focus – occupational risks in the energy sector
For pupils on the electrical engineering stream, the training focused on recognising the serious occupational risks characteristic of the technical and energy sectors, which are among the more demanding and hazardous working environments.
The topics covered included:
⚡ electric shock and contact with live installations;
🔥 fire and explosion hazards;
🏗️ work at height;
🛠️ injuries when working with tools and equipment;
⚙️ mechanical injuries;
👂 exposure to noise and vibration;
🧪 potential exposure to harmful substances;
🦺 the importance of personal protective equipment;
📋 risk assessment and safe working procedures.
It was emphasised that in technical occupations a single moment of inattention or failure to follow procedure can have serious consequences, which is why a culture of prevention and responsibility must be an integral part of the professional identity of future workers in this sector.
Active participation and interest among the pupils
One particularly notable aspect of this workshop was the high level of interest among the participants and their active involvement throughout the training.
The pupils showed great interest in practical topics directly connected to their future occupations, asked concrete questions and took an active part in the discussion.
It was especially evident that pupils recognise the importance of safety at work when it is presented in the context of their particular profession and of real situations from practice.
Support mechanisms for young people
The workshop also presented the help-line service / online Legal corner for young people, a support mechanism through which young people can obtain information, advice and expert support on labour rights and occupational safety and health.
It was emphasised that the aim of this service is to give young people a safe and accessible channel of support, particularly when they encounter uncertainties, irregularities or possible violations of their rights.
The workshop held at the Secondary School of Electrical Engineering and Economics in Bijelo Polje was a particularly valuable activity under the project, precisely because it successfully connected general topics of labour rights and decent work with the specific occupational risks relevant to the participants' different educational profiles.
The large number of participants, the quality of the interaction and the pupils' evident interest confirm that there is a real need for educational content of this kind in secondary vocational schools, so that young people gain the knowledge that will allow them a safer, better-informed and more responsible entry into the labour market.
This activity confirms once again that investing in young people's knowledge is one of the most important preventive measures in building a safer, healthier and more dignified working environment for future generations.
The project is financed by the European Union and co-financed by the Ministry of Regional Investment Development and Cooperation with Non-Governmental Organisations, within the YHWBT network.

