Dana 11. maja 2026. godine, u JU Srednja medicinska škola „Dr Branko Zogović“ realizovana je peta edukativna radionica u okviru projekta „Glas mladih za dostojanstven rad – od znanja do promjene“, usmjerena na jačanje znanja i svijesti mladih o radnim pravima, dostojanstvenom radu, zaštiti i zdravlju na radu, kao i pripremi mladih za sigurniji i odgovorniji ulazak na tržište rada.
Radionici su prisustvovala 24 učenika, koji se kroz svoje obrazovanje pripremaju za budući profesionalni angažman u zdravstvenom sektoru — oblasti koja nosi specifične profesionalne izazove, visoke zahtjeve i brojne rizike po bezbjednost i zdravlje zaposlenih. Edukaciju su realizovale Đina Janković i Sanja Dujović iz Udruženja zaštite na radu Crne Gore, kroz interaktivan, participativan i stručno utemeljen pristup koji je omogućio aktivno uključivanje učenika i otvorenu razmjenu iskustava i mišljenja.
Značaj radionice u kontekstu zdravstvenog sektora
Za razliku od prethodnih radionica koje su bile usmjerene na učenike različitih obrazovnih profila, ova edukativna aktivnost imala je poseban značaj upravo zbog specifičnosti zdravstvene struke. Učenici medicinske škole se tokom obrazovanja intenzivno pripremaju za rad sa pacijentima, usvajajući znanja i vještine koje se odnose na njegu, liječenje, profesionalnu odgovornost i zaštitu zdravlja drugih. Međutim, jedan od ključnih izazova koji je prepoznat jeste činjenica da se znatno manje pažnje posvećuje edukaciji o tome kako da zaštite sebe, svoje zdravlje i sopstvenu bezbjednost tokom profesionalnog rada.
Upravo iz tog razloga, ova radionica imala je dodatnu vrijednost, jer je otvorila prostor za razgovor o profesionalnim rizicima u sektoru zdravstva — oblasti koja se često doživljava isključivo kroz prizmu brige o pacijentima, dok se bezbjednost zdravstvenih radnika nerijetko stavlja u drugi plan.
Posebno je naglašeno da zdravstveni sektor predstavlja jedno od profesionalnih okruženja sa značajnim nivoom rizika, uključujući:
• biološke rizike i izloženost infektivnim agensima;
• kontakt sa potencijalno opasnim medicinskim materijalima;
• rizike od povreda oštrim predmetima i ubodnih incidenata;
• fizičko opterećenje usljed podizanja i premještanja pacijenata;
• psihofizički zamor, stres i emocionalno opterećenje;
• rad u smjenama i dugotrajna izloženost profesionalnom pritisku;
• psihosocijalne rizike, uključujući burnout i profesionalno iscrpljivanje.
Istaknuto je da kultura zaštite i zdravlja na radu mora podrazumijevati jednaku brigu i o zaposlenima koji pružaju zdravstvenu zaštitu, jer bez zaštite zdravstvenih radnika nema ni kvalitetne zaštite pacijenata.
Edukativni sadržaj radionice
Tokom radionice učenicima su predstavljene ključne teme iz oblasti dostojanstvenog rada, radnih prava i zaštite i zdravlja na radu.
U uvodnom dijelu predstavljen je projekat „Glas mladih za dostojanstven rad – od znanja do promjene“, njegovi ciljevi i značaj rada sa mladima u procesu njihove pripreme za tržište rada. Posebno je naglašeno da mladi često prvi kontakt sa radnim pravima i bezbjednošću na radu ostvaruju tek kada se suoče sa konkretnim problemima, što ih čini dodatno ranjivim.
U okviru edukativnog segmenta obrađene su teme:
• koncept dostojanstvenog rada;
• osnovna prava i obaveze iz radnog odnosa;
• značaj formalnog radnog angažovanja i ugovora o radu;
• zaštita i zdravlje na radu kao osnovno pravo zaposlenih;
• identifikacija profesionalnih rizika;
• preventivne mjere i odgovorno ponašanje na radnom mjestu.
Poseban fokus stavljen je na činjenicu da mladi, posebno u profesijama koje nose snažnu etičku komponentu poput zdravstvene, često razvijaju osjećaj odgovornosti prema drugima mnogo prije nego svijest o sopstvenoj zaštiti.
Aktivno učešće i izuzetna interakcija
Jedan od najznačajnijih aspekata ove radionice bio je izuzetno visok nivo zainteresovanosti učesnika i kvalitet interakcije tokom cijelog trajanja edukacije.
Učenici su pokazali visok stepen angažovanosti, aktivno učestvovali u diskusiji, postavljali brojna konkretna pitanja i povezivali predstavljene teme sa sopstvenim obrazovanjem i budućim profesionalnim iskustvima.
Posebno interesovanje izazvale su teme koje se odnose na:
• zaštitu zdravstvenih radnika od profesionalnih rizika;
• bezbjedno postupanje u kontaktu sa pacijentima;
• profesionalni stres i psihičko opterećenje u zdravstvenim profesijama;
• odgovornost poslodavca u obezbjeđivanju sigurnih uslova rada;
• prava mladih prilikom prvog zaposlenja.
Otvorenost učenika za razgovor i spremnost da aktivno učestvuju u analizi problema dodatno su potvrdili koliko je ova tema relevantna upravo za mlade koji se pripremaju za rad u sektoru zdravstva.
Mehanizmi podrške i osnaživanje mladih
U okviru radionice učenicima je predstavljen i Help-line servis / online Pravni kutak za mlade, kao mehanizam podrške koji omogućava mladima da se informišu, postavljaju pitanja i dobiju stručne smjernice u vezi sa radnim pravima i pitanjima zaštite i zdravlja na radu.
Naglašeno je da informisanost predstavlja jedan od ključnih mehanizama prevencije i zaštite, posebno za mlade koji tek započinju profesionalni put.
Radionica održana u JU Srednja medicinska škola „Dr Branko Zogović“ predstavljala je posebno značajnu aktivnost u okviru projekta, upravo zbog specifičnosti ciljne grupe i profesionalnog konteksta u kojem će se učesnici u budućnosti kretati.
Ova edukacija pokazala je da postoji realna potreba da se mladi koji se obrazuju za rad u zdravstvenom sektoru ne pripremaju samo za zaštitu pacijenata, već i za očuvanje sopstvenog zdravlja, bezbjednosti i profesionalnog integriteta.
Izuzetna zainteresovanost učesnika, kvalitet diskusije i visok nivo interakcije potvrđuju da mladi prepoznaju značaj ovih tema kada im se pruži prostor za otvoren, stručan i praktično usmjeren razgovor.
Zaključno, realizacija ove radionice predstavlja važan doprinos razvoju preventivne kulture među budućim zdravstvenim radnicima i još jednom potvrđuje da je ulaganje u znanje mladih jedan od najvažnijih koraka ka stvaranju sigurnijeg, odgovornijeg i dostojanstvenijeg radnog okruženja.
Projekat finansira Evropska unija, a kofinansira Ministarstvo regionalno-investicionog razvoja i saradnje sa nevladinim organizacijama, u okviru mreže YHWBT.

content_en:
On 11 May 2026, at the “Dr Branko Zogović” Secondary Medical School, the fifth educational workshop under the project “Youth voices for decent work – from knowledge to change” was held, aimed at strengthening young people's knowledge and awareness of labour rights, decent work and occupational safety and health, and at preparing them for a safer and more responsible entry into the labour market.
The workshop was attended by 24 pupils who, through their education, are preparing for future professional work in the health sector — a field that carries particular professional challenges, high demands and numerous risks to the safety and health of those who work in it. The training was delivered by Đina Janković and Sanja Dujović of the Occupational Safety Association of Montenegro, through an interactive, participatory and professionally grounded approach that allowed the pupils to take an active part and to exchange experiences and views openly.
The importance of the workshop in the context of the health sector
Unlike earlier workshops aimed at pupils from various educational profiles, this activity had a particular significance precisely because of the distinctive nature of the health professions. Pupils at a medical school are intensively prepared during their education for work with patients, acquiring knowledge and skills relating to care, treatment, professional responsibility and the protection of other people's health. One of the key challenges identified, however, is that far less attention is paid to teaching them how to protect themselves, their own health and their own safety in the course of their professional work.
For precisely that reason this workshop had added value, because it opened up space to talk about occupational risks in the health sector — a field often seen solely through the prism of caring for patients, while the safety of health workers is frequently left in the background.
It was particularly emphasised that the health sector is one of the professional environments with a significant level of risk, including:
• biological risks and exposure to infectious agents;
• contact with potentially hazardous medical materials;
• the risk of injuries from sharp objects and needlestick incidents;
• physical strain from lifting and moving patients;
• physical and mental fatigue, stress and emotional strain;
• shift work and prolonged exposure to professional pressure;
• psychosocial risks, including burnout and professional exhaustion.
It was stressed that a culture of occupational safety and health must mean equal care for the employees who provide health care, because without protection for health workers there can be no good protection for patients either.
The content of the workshop
During the workshop the pupils were introduced to key topics in decent work, labour rights and occupational safety and health.
The opening part presented the project “Youth voices for decent work – from knowledge to change”, its aims and the importance of working with young people as they prepare for the labour market. It was particularly emphasised that young people often come into contact with labour rights and safety at work for the first time only when they face a concrete problem, which leaves them all the more vulnerable.
The educational segment covered:
• the concept of decent work;
• basic rights and obligations arising from an employment relationship;
• the importance of formal engagement and of an employment contract;
• occupational safety and health as a basic right of employees;
• identifying occupational risks;
• preventive measures and responsible behaviour at the workplace.
Particular focus was placed on the fact that young people, especially in professions with a strong ethical dimension such as health care, often develop a sense of responsibility towards others long before any awareness of protecting themselves.
Active participation and outstanding interaction
One of the most significant aspects of this workshop was the exceptionally high level of interest among the participants and the quality of the interaction throughout the training.
The pupils showed a high degree of engagement, took an active part in the discussion, asked a great many concrete questions and connected the topics presented to their own education and future professional experience.
Particular interest was aroused by topics relating to:
• protecting health workers from occupational risks;
• safe practice in contact with patients;
• professional stress and mental strain in the health professions;
• the employer's responsibility to provide safe working conditions;
• young people's rights in their first job.
The pupils' openness to discussion and their willingness to take an active part in analysing the problems further confirmed how relevant this subject is for young people preparing to work in the health sector.
Support mechanisms and empowering young people
The workshop also presented the help-line service / online Legal corner for young people, a support mechanism that allows young people to find information, ask questions and receive expert guidance on labour rights and occupational safety and health.
It was emphasised that being informed is one of the key mechanisms of prevention and protection, particularly for young people just starting out on their professional path.
The workshop held at the “Dr Branko Zogović” Secondary Medical School was a particularly significant activity under the project, precisely because of the distinctive nature of the target group and of the professional environment the participants will work in.
This training showed that there is a real need for young people training to work in the health sector to be prepared not only to protect patients, but also to preserve their own health, safety and professional integrity.
The participants' exceptional interest, the quality of the discussion and the high level of interaction confirm that young people recognise the importance of these topics when they are given space for an open, expert and practically oriented conversation.
In conclusion, this workshop is an important contribution to developing a culture of prevention among future health workers, and confirms once again that investing in young people's knowledge is one of the most important steps towards creating a safer, more responsible and more dignified working environment.
The project is financed by the European Union and co-financed by the Ministry of Regional Investment Development and Cooperation with Non-Governmental Organisations, within the YHWBT network.

