U prostorijama Udruženja zaštite na radu Crne Gore, 12. februara 2026. godine, održan je sastanak povodom razmatranja predloga novog člana Zakona o zaštiti i zdravlju na radu koji se odnosi na kolektivno osiguranje zaposlenih.
Sastanku su prisustvovali predstavnici Udruženja zaštite na radu Crne Gore, kao predlagača norme, potpredsjednik Branislav Šebek i projektna menadžerka Đina Janković, predstavnice Ministarstva rada, zapošljavanja i socijalnog dijaloga – Jovana Bošković i Maja Mijović iz Direktorata za zaštitu i zdravlje na radu, kao i predstavnici socijalnih partnera: Rumica Kostić iz Unije poslodavaca Crne Gore, Ivana Mihajlović iz Unije slobodnih sindikata Crne Gore i Vladimir Krsmanović iz Saveza sindikata Crne Gore.
Podsjećanja radi, član 30 važećeg (starog) Zakona o zaštiti i zdravlju na radu propisivao je obavezu poslodavca da zaposlene osigurava od povreda na radu, profesionalnih bolesti i bolesti u vezi sa radom, pri čemu su premije padale na teret poslodavca i određivale se u zavisnosti od nivoa rizika. Međutim, zakon nije precizno definisao minimalni iznos premije, što je u praksi dovelo do situacija da pojedini poslodavci plaćaju simbolične iznose (30 ili 50 centi mjesečno po zaposlenom), bez realnog obezbjeđenja adekvatne zaštite.
Predlogom novog člana 38 predviđa se:
• obavezno kolektivno osiguranje zaposlenih od povreda na radu, profesionalnih bolesti i bolesti u vezi sa radom;
• da premija pada na teret poslodavca i da se određuje u skladu sa nivoom rizika radnog mjesta;
• da premija ne može biti manja od 0,5% minimalne zarade do V nivoa kvalifikacija na mjesečnom nivou, što bi u ovom trenutku iznosilo 3 eura;
• da osiguravajuća društva 10% prihoda ostvarenog po osnovu naplaćenih premija uplaćuju na poseban račun Fonda za zaštitu i zdravlje na radu, kao organizacione jedinice Ministarstva;
• da se sredstva Fonda koriste u javnom interesu, za unapređenje sistema prevencije, edukaciju, analizu rizika i sprovođenje politika zaštite i zdravlja na radu;
• da bliži način organizacije i funkcionisanja Fonda propiše Vlada Crne Gore posebnim aktom.
Tokom sastanka razmatran je model kojim bi se osiguranje zaposlenih uredilo sistemski, uz jasno definisanu minimalnu premiju, kako bi se izbjegla dosadašnja praksa nedovoljnih uplata i formalnog ispunjavanja obaveze. Posebno je naglašeno da sistem ne treba da bude zasnovan isključivo na naknadi štete nakon nastanka povrede, već i na prevenciji, kroz izdvajanje dijela sredstava za unapređenje zaštite i zdravlja na radu.
Socijalni partneri izrazili su saglasnost sa predloženim rješenjem, uključujući i visinu minimalne premije. Ipak, otvoreno je pitanje da li će ovakav model u praksi dovesti do veće i pravičnije naknade za zaposlene u slučaju povrede na radu. Zaključeno je da će to zavisiti od načina na koji osiguravajuća društva obračunavaju i isplaćuju naknade, te da će dodatni sastanak sa predstavnicima osiguravajućih kuća biti ključan za preciziranje tog segmenta.
Posebno je istaknuto da je i dosadašnji član 30 Zakona nedvosmisleno propisivao obavezu osiguranja zaposlenih od povreda na radu, profesionalnih bolesti i bolesti u vezi sa radom, ali da je ta odredba godinama ostala neprimijenjena i bez ikakvog suštinskog efekta. U praksi, u Crnoj Gori nijedan poslodavac ne plaća osiguranje od profesionalnih bolesti niti od bolesti u vezi sa radom, već se osiguranje svodi isključivo na osiguranje od povreda na radu, odnosno tzv. „nezgode“ koje dovode do invaliditeta, kako ih klasifikuju osiguravajuća društva.
Ukazano je da osiguravajuće kuće ne nude osiguranje od profesionalnih bolesti jer država nije uspostavila referentnu medicinsku instituciju nadležnu za njihovo stručno utvrđivanje i verifikaciju. Dodatno, ne postoji zvanična lista bolesti u vezi sa radom, niti jasno definisan postupak njihovog dokazivanja, što ovu zakonsku kategoriju čini pravno neprimjenjivom. Time je zaposlenima faktički uskraćeno pravo koje im je zakonom formalno garantovano, a sistem zaštite i zdravlja na radu ostavljen bez ključnog mehanizma odgovornosti. Ovakvo stanje predstavlja ozbiljan institucionalni propust i potvrđuje da je neophodno hitno i cjelovito normativno i institucionalno uređenje ove oblasti, kako bi zakon imao stvarnu primjenu, a zaposleni efektivnu zaštitu.
Učesnici su ukazali i na potrebu jasnog definisanja obaveza osiguravajućih društava, načina kontrole, obračuna i uplate premija, kao i uspostavljanja transparentnog mehanizma upravljanja sredstvima Fonda uz nadzor nadležnog ministarstva.
Zaključeno je da je neophodno dodatno normativno preciziranje kako bi predloženi model bio održiv, pravilno primjenjiv i finansijski kontrolisan, te kako bi se obezbijedila stvarna zaštita zaposlenih i dugoročno unapređenje sistema zaštite i zdravlja na radu u Crnoj Gori.

content_en:
On 12 February 2026, at the premises of the Occupational Safety Association of Montenegro, a meeting was held to consider a proposed new article of the Law on Occupational Safety and Health concerning collective insurance for employees.
The meeting was attended by representatives of the Occupational Safety Association of Montenegro, as the proposer of the provision — vice-president Branislav Šebek and project manager Đina Janković — by representatives of the Ministry of Labour, Employment and Social Dialogue, Jovana Bošković and Maja Mijović of the Directorate for Occupational Safety and Health, and by representatives of the social partners: Rumica Kostić of the Employers' Union of Montenegro, Ivana Mihajlović of the Union of Free Trade Unions of Montenegro and Vladimir Krsmanović of the Confederation of Trade Unions of Montenegro.
By way of a reminder, Article 30 of the current (old) Law on Occupational Safety and Health required employers to insure employees against injuries at work, occupational diseases and work-related illnesses, with the premiums borne by the employer and set according to the level of risk. The law did not, however, define a minimum premium precisely, which in practice led to situations in which some employers pay token amounts (30 or 50 cents a month per employee), without providing any real protection.
The proposed new Article 38 provides for:
• compulsory collective insurance of employees against injuries at work, occupational diseases and work-related illnesses;
• the premium being borne by the employer and set according to the level of risk of the workplace;
• the premium being no less than 0.5% of the minimum wage up to qualification level V per month, which at present would amount to 3 euros;
• insurance companies paying 10% of the income earned from the premiums collected into a separate account of the Fund for Occupational Safety and Health, as an organisational unit of the Ministry;
• the Fund's resources being used in the public interest, to improve the prevention system, education, risk analysis and the implementation of occupational safety and health policy;
• the Government of Montenegro laying down the detailed organisation and functioning of the Fund in a separate act.
The meeting considered a model that would regulate employee insurance systematically, with a clearly defined minimum premium, so as to put an end to the practice of insufficient payments and of formally satisfying the obligation. Particular emphasis was placed on the fact that the system should not be based solely on compensating damage after an injury has occurred but also on prevention, by setting aside part of the funds to improve occupational safety and health.
The social partners expressed their agreement with the proposed solution, including the level of the minimum premium. It remains an open question, however, whether such a model will in practice lead to greater and fairer compensation for employees in the event of an injury at work. It was concluded that this will depend on how insurance companies calculate and pay compensation, and that a further meeting with representatives of the insurance companies will be crucial to settling that part.
It was particularly emphasised that the existing Article 30 of the Law also unambiguously required employees to be insured against injuries at work, occupational diseases and work-related illnesses, but that this provision has remained unapplied for years and without any real effect. In practice, in Montenegro no employer pays insurance against occupational diseases or work-related illnesses; insurance is confined solely to insurance against injuries at work, that is, to the so-called “accidents” leading to disability, as the insurance companies classify them.
It was pointed out that insurance companies do not offer insurance against occupational diseases because the state has not established a reference medical institution responsible for determining and verifying them professionally. In addition, there is no official list of work-related illnesses, nor any clearly defined procedure for proving them, which makes this legal category legally inapplicable. Employees are thereby effectively denied a right that the law formally guarantees them, and the occupational safety and health system is left without a key mechanism of accountability. This state of affairs is a serious institutional failure and confirms that urgent, comprehensive legislative and institutional regulation of this field is essential if the law is to be genuinely applied and employees are to be effectively protected.
Participants also pointed to the need to define clearly the obligations of insurance companies, the way premiums are controlled, calculated and paid, and to establish a transparent mechanism for managing the Fund's resources under the supervision of the competent ministry.
It was concluded that further legislative precision is needed if the proposed model is to be sustainable, properly applicable and financially controlled, and if employees are to be genuinely protected and the occupational safety and health system in Montenegro improved in the long term.

