Under the regional project “Improving working conditions and occupational safety and health in support of the Western Balkan countries in the EU accession process”, under a grant agreement supported and financed by the European Union, the BALcanOSH network invites all interested media organisations and journalists to join in forming a regional media network in the field of labour. The purpose of forming and running the regional network is the need to improve communication and cooperation between civil society organisations, media outlets and journalists in promoting issues relating to labour, working conditions and occupational safety and health in the Western Balkans. Beyond that, the network will aim to strengthen the stability of the countries and to accelerate their political, economic and social reform on the path of European integration. Specific objectives:
- Supporting the continuous improvement of journalistic independence and professionalism and strengthening the integrity of the media;
- Raising public awareness of human rights, and in particular of workers' rights and of reducing the number of injuries at work and occupational diseases;
- Establishing cooperation and close partnership between regional media organisations, civil society and the BALcanOSH network, all with the aim of improving working conditions and occupational safety and health in the countries of the Western Balkans.
In this way at least fifteen journalists from North Macedonia, Serbia, Kosovo, Albania and Montenegro will be involved.
The journalists selected will have the opportunity to take part in the following activities:
A workshop on occupational safety and health and on standards of investigative journalism and reporting;
Becoming members of and working within an informal media network, preceded by the signing of a memorandum of cooperation;
Supporting the preparation of media stories and articles and their sharing and publication at regional level;
Taking part in occasional consultative meetings of the network of media organisations;
Taking part in the development of an innovative IT solution to serve as a support tool in the network's work;
Being involved in the project's promotional activities;
Having the opportunity to take part in the support scheme financed under this project.
An application nominating the person who will take part in the activities of the regional media network in the field of labour and occupational safety and health, together with a short CV, should be sent to kontakt@mzzpr.org.mk
Please nominate your representatives by 8 October 2021 at 17:00 at the latest.
You will find the application form at the link attached.
Yours sincerely,
Project coordinator Đina Janković
LINK

On 16 February 2021, at the Hotel Terminus, the Occupational Safety Association of Montenegro held the kick-off meeting of the project “Build knowledge – protect yourself”, financed by the Ministry of Economic Development (formerly the Ministry of Labour and Social Welfare). The meeting, at which the planned activities were presented, was attended by representatives of employers, employees, public institutions, OSH professionals and the media.
Construction is one of the largest industrial sectors worldwide but also one of the riskiest for employees' lives and health. Employees in construction are exposed to a high risk of injury at work, particularly serious and fatal injuries, which is of great concern to the International Labour Organization and the European Agency for Safety and Health at Work (EU-OSHA), and also to public and private institutions in Montenegro and to employees' representatives and occupational safety and health specialists — given that one of the benchmarks for closing Chapter 19 is implementation of the Law on Occupational Safety and Health, which in its most recent amendments was aligned with the relevant EU directives, with particular emphasis on construction.
Through the workshops and training planned, the preparation of leaflets for workers on construction sites, guidance and instructions for the work of labour inspectors in occupational safety and health, training for site coordinators (K1 and K2) by international ILO experts, awareness campaigns, a call for awards in OSH, a conference to mark World Day for Safety and Health at Work on 28 April, visits to construction sites to train workers, and numerous panel discussions for safety professionals, coordinators and public institutions, the Occupational Safety Association of Montenegro will seek to raise the awareness of employers and employees regarding occupational safety and health on construction sites.
The general aim of the project is to address the problems identified in order to reduce the number of injuries at work in the construction sector — serious, collective and fatal — and of occupational diseases and work-related illnesses. This will be achieved by:
- Building the capacity of managers and strengthening the quality of work and the role of safety professionals, who have both obligations and responsibilities in implementing occupational safety and health measures in the construction sector, through a panel discussion organised to that end. At the panel discussion they will gain practical knowledge that will help them recognise more easily the concrete problems faced by employees of various qualifications and specialisms working on temporary and mobile construction sites, and will therefore be able to take more effective preventive measures through implementing the Law on Occupational Safety and Health in construction — one of the benchmarks for closing Chapter 19 — which will lead to fewer injuries;
- Improving the work of labour inspectors for occupational safety and health, who are the main regulatory instrument for enforcing the Law on Occupational Safety and Health. Besides a lack of equipment, another problem for the labour inspectorate is that it has only a limited number of inspectors with the technical and expert knowledge needed for site inspections. The technical capacity of occupational safety and health inspectors must therefore be strengthened so that integrated services and a greater number of inspections can be provided with the resources available. Given the high number of injuries at work in construction, training on site inspection is planned for OSH labour inspectors, focusing particularly on small and micro sites, to be delivered by experts from the region and Europe, from the International Labour Organization. The aim of these sessions will be to acquaint labour inspectors for occupational safety and health with the skills needed to carry out effective inspections of working conditions and of occupational safety and health in construction. Through this training they will gain practical knowledge and learn how to develop a methodology for inspecting construction work from the planning stage to the submission of the inspection report. A call will then be issued to engage an expert in the field, who will prepare a Guide for labour inspectors for OSH; the published guide will serve inspectors as guidance enabling proactive visits, since inspection visits play a decisive role in preventing accidents and health problems on sites, as stated in Article 35(b) of ILO Convention No. 167 of 1988 on safety and health in construction;
- Building employees' capacity, since there is a great unmet need to strengthen occupational safety and health capacity, among employers and employees alike, so that OSH measures can be applied more effectively. This will be achieved through training and by engaging competent specialists in the field. Emphasis will be placed in particular on educating employees with a low level of education, among whom injuries most often occur, through practical training on site, where a team of OSH specialists will acquaint them with their rights, obligations and responsibilities. (For example, through this practical training on site they will learn about the most common dangers during ordinary work, including work at height — since falls account for a high proportion of accidents on sites and, by their very nature, a great many of those accidents result in serious and even fatal injuries. Accordingly, all employees and their work organisers will receive information and training on risk perception and safe working practice as regards work at height, and on the importance of using personal protective equipment as a protective measure to prevent exposure to danger, all in line with the Rulebook on occupational safety and health measures on temporary and mobile construction sites.) In addition, 1,500 leaflets will be prepared and printed setting out, in pictures, basic information about the greatest risks of injury on a construction site, to be distributed to workers in the construction sector;
- Building the capacity of future occupational safety and health coordinators at the design stage and during construction, since it is of key importance that coordinators be as well trained as possible so that they can find the best available solutions in construction technology and occupational safety and health. To that end, one of the project's activities will be to organise consultations and expert visits by eminent specialists from the region (Serbia, Slovenia) who will share their experience of coordinators' work, offer any proposals and guidance and point to the problems and challenges they encountered when introducing these practices in their own countries and how best to resolve them. This will contribute to better implementation of the Law on Occupational Safety and Health in construction, one of the benchmarks for closing Chapter 19, and thereby to fewer injuries, particularly serious and fatal ones.
- Through a campaign promoting occupational safety and health, which will include a call for awards to individuals and organisations that have made the greatest contribution in this field in the construction sector, and by marking World Day for Safety and Health at Work in this way, attention will be drawn to good practice that will encourage and serve as an example to others in meeting the legal obligations and responsibilities employers have under the Law on Occupational Safety and Health.
Achieving these objectives will serve the public interest by reducing the economic losses caused by injuries at work, occupational diseases and work-related illnesses (sick leave, early and disability pensions). Through the exchange and promotion of good practice and the recognition of socially responsible companies, attention will be drawn to good practice, and employers who invest in the safety and health of their employees — who are in fact every company's most important capital — will encourage and serve as an example to others, which will help raise collective awareness.

